WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.700
How can one missed separation give one gamete extra chromosome material and another

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:11.660
too little? Watch one replicated chromosome pair cross both meiotic divisions.

3
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.040
Each homologue has joined sister chromatids.

4
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.080
Blue marks one homologue, and gold marks the other.

5
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.040
The centromeres remain visible as small circles.

6
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.220
At meiosis I, homologues normally move apart.

7
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.040
At meiosis II, sisters normally separate.

8
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.080
The first division sorts homologues; the second sorts copies within each homologue.

9
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.033
Nondisjunction means chromosomes fail to part during a division.

10
00:00:46.253 --> 00:00:51.033
The resulting gamete may carry an extra chromosome or none of that chromosome.

11
00:00:52.393 --> 00:00:57.853
This is aneuploidy, a gain or loss of individual chromosomes rather than a whole

12
00:00:57.853 --> 00:00:58.853
set.

13
00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:05.517
First, let both homologues travel together at meiosis I. One daughter cell receives

14
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:08.857
blue and gold. The other receives neither.

15
00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:15.257
Their sister chromatids remain joined, so the first division has already created a

16
00:01:15.257 --> 00:01:16.257
dose imbalance.

17
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.750
Now suppose meiosis II runs normally.

18
00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.890
Can separating the sisters repair the chromosome dose, or is the final pattern

19
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:26.890
already fixed?

20
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:36.087
The later division cannot replace a missing homologue.

21
00:01:37.487 --> 00:01:41.667
Normal sister separation produces matching outcomes within each branch.

22
00:01:43.027 --> 00:01:45.727
The products from one branch carry both homologues.

23
00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:48.607
The products from the other carry neither.

24
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:51.027
The counts appear on screen.

25
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:58.220
Reset to normal meiosis I. Blue and gold separate into different daughter cells.

26
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.500
During meiosis II, blue sisters fail to part in only one cell.

27
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.920
Gold sisters still separate normally in the other cell.

28
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:12.010
This error makes three dose classes instead of two.

29
00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:18.130
One product has extra material, one is missing it, and the remaining products are

30
00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:22.950
normal. The stage of the missed separation sets the product pattern.

31
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.270
It also leaves a fingerprint that DNA markers in the child can read.

32
00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:33.133
A normal gamete contributes one copy at fertilization.

33
00:02:34.413 --> 00:02:38.413
A gamete with extra material can therefore make a trisomic zygote.

34
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:43.513
A gamete missing that chromosome can make a monosomic zygote.

35
00:02:44.693 --> 00:02:47.593
That starting dose is copied as the zygote divides.

36
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:53.833
Extra or missing chromosome material changes the copies of many genes together.

37
00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:57.373
That can alter development and survival.

38
00:02:58.773 --> 00:03:02.373
Most aneuploid human embryos do not survive pregnancy.

39
00:03:03.853 --> 00:03:09.273
Trisomy 21 is among the few autosomal trisomies seen regularly at birth.

40
00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:13.307
The division error explains the product pattern.

41
00:03:14.987 --> 00:03:17.967
DNA dose explains why the pattern matters biologically.

42
00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:24.007
Keep those questions separate: where did separation fail, and what chromosome

43
00:03:24.007 --> 00:03:25.967
material became extra or missing?

44
00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:29.827
The same count can hide different routes to imbalance.
